  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1221 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nFor the week ahead, the main forecast concerns are associated with\na low pressure system, currently moving southeast along the West\ncoast, merging with the remnants of tropical system Polo over the\nDesert Southwest and lifting into the southern/central Plains\nmid-late week. With this system, an increasing heavy rain threat\nis developing for the second half of the week along with a\ncontinued limited severe potential.\n\nLatest model solutions are in general agreement with the merging\nof these two features over the Desert Southwest Tuesday/Wednesday.\nHowever, beyond Wednesday model run to run solutions continue\nindicate uncertainties in the timing and track of the low \npressure system through the Plains. The general track of the \nsystem could depend on a wave/trof axis moving across the northern\nPlains with a trailing cold front approaching the southern Plains\nlate week. This could hinder the northern extent of the low \npressure system lifting into the southern Plains, and keep a \nweakening low pressure over the region into the weekend. If part \nor most of the low pressure system can remain ahead of the trof \naxis, then a quicker exit of the low pressure system is possible.\nFor now will continue with a blended approach with a lean toward \na slower solution that could keep precip chances going into the \nweekend.\n\nFor this forecast, a slight chance of showers/storms could develop\nwest of Highway 75 Tuesday afternoon as the first slug of moisture\nlifts north/northeast through west and central Oklahoma. The main\nmoisture plume is currently progged to lift into the region\nWednesday with the low pressure system near the Oklahoma Texas\nPanhandles. In response, rain showers are forecast to become\nlikely from west to east, especially Wednesday night through\nFriday. Overall instability tries to spread eastward with the \nmoisture, though at this time the eastern periphery of the \ninstability axis looks to only get into a portion of eastern \nOklahoma. Thus, will continue with low chances for thunderstorms \nfor eastern Oklahoma and isolated chances for northwest Arkansas.\nIncreasing winds aloft could aid in limited severe potentials\nWednesday/Thursday. \n \nHigh moisture content with precipitable water values near \nclimatological max for the time of year looks to be the main\nthreat with this multi-day precipitation forecast. As of this\nforecast, Wednesday night into Friday is the general window for \nmultiple periods of heavy rainfall. Overall recent antecedent \nconditions have been dry for much of the CWA. However, for any \nprecip that can train over the same locations with efficient rain \nproducing cells could create localized flooding impacts. With the \nuncertainties mentioned above, rainfall amounts and their \nlocations will continue to be revised as the event approaches. \nLastly, rain and storm chances for this forecast hold on into the \nweekend for portions of the CWA before the remaining form of the \nlow pressure system weakens and or exits. \n\nTemperatures for the week ahead remain above seasonal average \ninto Wednesday, and then fall back into the 70s/80s for highs \nlate week into the weekend.",
